1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,684
Welcome to the IETF 127 Hackathon.

2
00:00:03,724 --> 00:00:10,478
This is our Single Bundle demonstration
of CoAP over Bundle Protocol version 7,

3
00:00:10,518 --> 00:00:18,218
following draft five, with RFC 9758 three-element
IPN addressing, and no proxy in between.

4
00:00:18,758 --> 00:00:23,735
It is championed onsite by Professor
Anna Calveras, and remotely by

5
00:00:23,775 --> 00:00:28,078
Mahitab S. Elattar, of the
Wireless Networks Group at UPC.

6
00:00:29,418 --> 00:00:31,666
The goal is simple and precise.

7
00:00:31,706 --> 00:00:35,504
First, the Rover sends one
non-confirmable CoAP GET.

8
00:00:36,044 --> 00:00:41,616
Second, the Satellite forwards it as a pure
bundle relay, with no CoAP proxy and no

9
00:00:41,656 --> 00:00:43,171
application processing.

10
00:00:43,711 --> 00:00:49,758
Third, the Earth CoAP server receives the
request and answers in a separate BPv7 bundle.

11
00:00:50,298 --> 00:00:56,842
And fourth, the Rover matches that 2.05 Content
response to its request, using the CoAP token.

12
00:00:58,182 --> 00:00:59,779
Here is the architecture.

13
00:00:59,819 --> 00:01:04,887
Think of it as a deep-space postal
service, built from three virtual machines:

14
00:01:04,927 --> 00:01:06,983
a Rover, a Satellite, and Earth.

15
00:01:07,523 --> 00:01:13,750
The Rover writes a CoAP request, seals it
inside a BPv7 bundle, and sends it over

16
00:01:13,790 --> 00:01:16,612
the TCP Convergence Layer, version 3.

17
00:01:17,152 --> 00:01:20,028
The Satellite behaves like a sorting office.

18
00:01:20,068 --> 00:01:26,390
It reads only the address on the envelope, the
destination endpoint ID, and forwards the bundle.

19
00:01:26,430 --> 00:01:28,113
It never opens the letter:

20
00:01:28,153 --> 00:01:30,962
there is no CoAP layer on this node at all.

21
00:01:31,502 --> 00:01:36,625
Earth opens the bundle, processes the CoAP
request, and sends its reply back to the

22
00:01:36,665 --> 00:01:38,367
Rover in a brand-new bundle.

23
00:01:39,707 --> 00:01:49,783
Every endpoint here has an address in the IPN
scheme, and RFC 9758 gives that address three levels.

24
00:01:49,823 --> 00:01:54,994
Take the Rover Safety
application: ipn:974848.1.10.

25
00:01:55,534 --> 00:01:58,596
The first level is the allocator, 974848.

26
00:01:58,636 --> 00:02:04,799
It identifies the organization that hands
out node numbers, a bit like the country

27
00:02:04,839 --> 00:02:06,539
code of a phone number.

28
00:02:07,079 --> 00:02:09,449
The second level is the node number, 1.

29
00:02:09,489 --> 00:02:15,319
Within that allocator, it identifies one machine,
here the Rover, like the phone number itself.

30
00:02:15,858 --> 00:02:18,704
The third level is the service number, 10.

31
00:02:18,744 --> 00:02:23,857
It selects one application on that
node, like an extension on a phone line.

32
00:02:23,897 --> 00:02:27,155
Service 10 is Safety, and
service 11 is Science.

33
00:02:27,695 --> 00:02:29,275
So, from left to right:

34
00:02:29,315 --> 00:02:33,640
who assigned the number, which
machine, and which application.

35
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:39,414
Earth's CoAP server is node 3, service 64,
and the Satellite is node 2, service 0,

36
00:02:39,454 --> 00:02:41,385
its administrative endpoint.

37
00:02:42,725 --> 00:02:47,769
The same three numbers appear in three places.

38
00:02:47,809 --> 00:02:53,294
As text, Earth's CoAP
endpoint is ipn:974848.3.64.

39
00:02:53,834 --> 00:02:59,488
On the wire, the bundle encodes it in CBOR:
scheme code 2, for IPN, followed by an

40
00:02:59,528 --> 00:03:01,154
array of three elements:

41
00:03:01,194 --> 00:03:03,167
allocator, node, and service.

42
00:03:03,707 --> 00:03:11,768
And inside CoAP, the Uri-Host option
carries the same address written in reverse,

43
00:03:11,808 --> 00:03:15,768
like a domain name: 64.3.974848.ipn.alt.

44
00:03:15,808 --> 00:03:19,268
Service, then node, then allocator.

45
00:03:20,608 --> 00:03:22,362
Now, the request itself.

46
00:03:22,402 --> 00:03:27,743
The Rover Safety application sends a
non-confirmable GET to the resource

47
00:03:27,783 --> 00:03:29,611
/mission/safety-guidance.

48
00:03:30,151 --> 00:03:39,319
It carries a 24-bit message ID and the
token 684d4727, inside a 59-byte BP payload.

49
00:03:39,858 --> 00:03:46,005
This is exactly one CoAP message in one bundle,
so the Payload-length option is correctly absent.

50
00:03:47,345 --> 00:03:55,964
In Mission Control, the Rover Safety
application is registered at ipn:974848.1.10.

51
00:03:56,504 --> 00:04:02,441
Its last exchange returned 2.05 Content
with a round-trip time of 72 milliseconds,

52
00:04:02,481 --> 00:04:07,032
and all four Safety GET replies
were matched: four out of four.

53
00:04:08,372 --> 00:04:12,940
On Earth, a live Wireshark capture
shows the request as frame 14.

54
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:23,993
The bundle travels from ipn:974848.1.10
to ipn:974848.3.64, carried over the TCP

55
00:04:24,033 --> 00:04:27,819
Convergence Layer, version 3.

56
00:04:28,359 --> 00:04:35,283
Inside it, our CoAP over BP dissector
decodes one GET: type non-confirmable, code

57
00:04:35,323 --> 00:04:38,378
0.01, with its message ID and token.

58
00:04:38,918 --> 00:04:43,895
The options show the Uri-Host and the
two Uri-Path segments, mission and

59
00:04:43,935 --> 00:04:47,100
safety-guidance, and no Payload-length option.

60
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:54,304
For this baseline, the Rover requested a
bundle lifetime of 100 seconds, that is

61
00:04:54,344 --> 00:04:58,880
100,000 milliseconds, and the
test logs report the same value.

62
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:04,693
NSTART stays at its default of one, so there
is only one outstanding request at a time.

63
00:05:05,233 --> 00:05:07,384
And the message framing is Single:

64
00:05:07,424 --> 00:05:10,218
one non-confirmable CoAP message per bundle.

65
00:05:11,558 --> 00:05:16,304
Earth answers in a separate bundle:

66
00:05:16,344 --> 00:05:25,330
frame 31, travelling back from
ipn:974848.3.64 to ipn:974848.1.10.

67
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:33,063
The response is non-confirmable, with code
2.05 Content, in a 103-byte BP payload.

68
00:05:33,603 --> 00:05:40,487
And most importantly, it carries the
same token as the request: 684d4727.

69
00:05:41,827 --> 00:05:46,462
This is how a non-confirmable
exchange stays connected.

70
00:05:46,502 --> 00:05:50,628
The request leaves the
Rover with token 684d4727.

71
00:05:51,168 --> 00:05:57,529
The response comes back with a different
message ID, 239, but with the very same token.

72
00:05:58,069 --> 00:06:04,025
The token, not the message ID, ties the answer
to the question, and the token match passes.

73
00:06:05,365 --> 00:06:09,983
The Earth server log tells the
same story at application level.

74
00:06:10,023 --> 00:06:15,454
It received a 59-byte ADU from the
Rover, and parsed a Single CoAP GET for

75
00:06:15,494 --> 00:06:17,302
/mission/safety-guidance.

76
00:06:17,842 --> 00:06:31,123
It addressed the response from ipn:974848.3.64
back to ipn:974848.1.10, with code 2.05 Content.

77
00:06:31,663 --> 00:06:37,577
It sent a Single non-confirmable response,
and the requested lifetime check, 100,000

78
00:06:37,617 --> 00:06:39,066
milliseconds, passed.

79
00:06:40,406 --> 00:06:47,061
We also checked the IPN encoding independently,
in a companion capture from the same

80
00:06:47,101 --> 00:06:48,337
prototype build.

81
00:06:48,377 --> 00:06:50,648
Scheme code 2 identifies IPN.

82
00:06:51,188 --> 00:06:52,694
The element count is 3:

83
00:06:52,734 --> 00:06:56,322
allocator, node, and service,
each encoded separately.

84
00:06:56,862 --> 00:07:02,749
Both endpoints show allocator 974848,
which Wireshark labels as the Example

85
00:07:02,789 --> 00:07:07,016
allocator, each with its own
node and service numbers.

86
00:07:08,356 --> 00:07:10,358
Here are the observed results.

87
00:07:10,398 --> 00:07:14,713
Four of four Safety GET replies,
from the GUI and the Rover log.

88
00:07:15,253 --> 00:07:19,623
A 59-byte Single request
payload, in Wireshark frame 14.

89
00:07:20,163 --> 00:07:23,339
A 2.05 Content response, in frame 31.

90
00:07:23,879 --> 00:07:27,854
And a token match, confirmed by
both the Earth and the Rover logs.

91
00:07:29,194 --> 00:07:31,319
To be precise about scope:

92
00:07:31,359 --> 00:07:37,981
this test verifies a direct non-confirmable
Single GET, TCPCL version 3 and BPv7

93
00:07:38,021 --> 00:07:45,643
transport, CoAP response and token matching,
and three-element IPN in the companion capture.

94
00:07:46,183 --> 00:07:51,747
Still to validate are delayed or disrupted
contacts, congestion and loss scenarios,

95
00:07:51,787 --> 00:07:56,271
wider draft-five interoperability,
and final standards conformance.

96
00:07:57,611 --> 00:07:58,475
In summary:

97
00:07:58,515 --> 00:08:04,886
one CoAP non-confirmable GET, one BPv7
request bundle, and one matching reply.

98
00:08:05,426 --> 00:08:06,775
Thank you for watching.

99
00:08:06,815 --> 00:08:11,489
We welcome your feedback from the CoRE
and DTN working groups, and from fellow

100
00:08:11,529 --> 00:08:12,878
Hackathon participants.
